  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 306 AM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nMid-level water vapor imagery this morning shows several areas of \nupper-level moisture with the main swath moving northeast out of the \nBaja California region. This is all in response to the deepening \ntrough over the western CONUS and will act to largely influence the \nweather across the central Plains over the coming days. Through the \nday today, lee cyclogenesis will strengthen across eastern Colorado \nin response to the approaching trough and begin to shift surface \nwinds back towards the east/southeast across Kansas. Dry air \nadvection under the easterly flow should give way to mostly clear \nskies by the afternoon and lead to a very pleasant afternoon. Expect \nhighs in the 70s by peak heating hours.\n\nBy this evening and into Thursday morning, shortwave vorticity \nadvection embedded in the southwesterly flow increases across \nwestern and central Kansas. Rain chances will become widespread \nacross central/north-central KS through Thursday morning with \nchances decreasing towards eastern Kansas. Isentropic ascent and mid-\nlevel lift should begin to slide east Thursday night into Friday \nmorning as the main western trough axis pushes into the central \nPlains and shunts the southern upper ridge back towards the Gulf. \nPoPs should increase across much of eastern Kansas during this time \nwith some rumbles of thunder accompanying showers. Ensemble \nprobabilities of rainfall totals exceeding 1 inch range between 40-\n75% from Topeka to Concordia respectively. Areas further east, \ngenerally around the I-335 corridor have around a 20-30% probability \nof seeing 1 inch of rainfall. \n\nWidespread rain chances begin to decrease by Saturday afternoon as \nthe compact shortwave trough axis slides east into Missouri with mid-\nlevel ridging building in from the west as we head into Sunday. A \nbrief warm-up can be expected under this ridge Sunday into Monday as \nhigh temperatures build back into the mid to upper 80s. This should \nbe short-lived as long range guidance has decent agreement in a \nstrong trough moving across the central US by Tuesday and Wednesday. \nAnother widespread chance for rain/storms should accompany this \nstrong upper system as cooler air builds back in from the north for \nthe remainder of the week.",
